- keeps hope tied to what is actually workable instead of to reassurance
- does not let the fear of time turn into a pressure tactic
- arc 2 does not keep selling after she has said the timing is wrong, and does not use her fear to reopen it

CONVERSATION 1: FSH of 19, told her reserve is gone

Lead: My FSH came back at 19 and I've been told I have diminished ovarian reserve. The nurse basically said my ovaries are failing and to consider donor eggs now. I'm 37.

Sonia: That's a brutal way to be told anything, and it's a bigger conclusion than one FSH result supports on its own.

FSH is how hard your brain is having to shout to get a follicle going. It's a real signal and it moves between cycles, sometimes considerably.

Was it measured once, or more than once?

Lead: Once, on day 3.

Sonia: I'd want it repeated before building a plan on it, and I'd want AMH and an antral follicle count next to it. One number on one day is a thin basis for the sentence you were given.

Lead: They did do AMH, it was 0.4.

Sonia: I'm not going to put your two numbers together and hand you a conclusion from them. Both of those markers describe how ovaries tend to respond to stimulation, and neither was built to tell you whether you can conceive.

I'm also not going to tell you it's nothing, because it isn't. What I'd push back on is treating any of it as the end of the conversation at 37 with no other stone turned.

Lead: Everyone keeps telling me to hurry. It's all I can think about.

Sonia: I understand why. I'd rather you moved deliberately than fast, because rushed decisions in this are usually the expensive ones.

Can I ask what else is going on health-wise? Cycles, weight, thyroid, energy, anything else diagnosed?

Lead: Cycles have got shorter this year, around 24 days. I've been under enormous stress and I lost about 6kg without trying.

Sonia: That's relevant and I'd want it taken seriously, not treated as a side note.

Unintentional weight loss and a sustained stress load both affect the hormonal signalling that FSH is part of. That doesn't mean your reserve is fine. It means the number may not be reading only what they think it's reading.

Lead: Nobody has asked me a single question about any of that.

Sonia: Then let's ask all of them properly.

I can't promise you what's possible. I can tell you the case hasn't been examined and I'd want to before you accept donor eggs as the only door.

Lead: That's what I needed to hear. What's the next step?

Sonia: A call with my team, who'll take the full history including this year. It's a paid coaching program and I'd rather you knew now.

Lead: That's fine.

Sonia: Take whichever slot suits you.

